<GuideArticle slug="why-aruba-is-outside-the-hurricane-belt"
  title="Why Aruba sits outside the hurricane belt"
  metaTitle="Is Aruba in the Hurricane Belt? Why It's the Caribbean's Safest Date"
  description="Aruba lies south of the Atlantic hurricane track, so direct hits are extremely rare. What that means for booking a wedding or trip 12 months ahead."
  kicker="Weather · planning ahead" faq={faq}>
  <p>If you're booking a date a year ahead, especially a wedding, this is the most important fact about Aruba: <strong>it sits outside the hurricane belt.</strong></p>
  <h2>The geography</h2>
  <p>Aruba lies at about 12.5° north, roughly 25 km off the coast of Venezuela, at the southern edge of the Caribbean. Atlantic hurricanes form off Africa and generally track west and northwest, passing well north of the island. As a result, direct hits on Aruba are extremely rare. The island's climate is also a desert one: steady trade winds, around 28°C (82°F) all year, and less than 500 mm (20 in) of rain annually.</p>
  <h2>What it means for planning</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Weddings:</strong> a date set 8–12 months out isn't a hurricane-season gamble, the way it is across most of the Caribbean.</li>
    <li><strong>September to November:</strong> the quietest, best-value months are safe, when hurricane season worries travellers elsewhere.</li>
    <li><strong>Insurance still matters:</strong> distant storms can occasionally bring clouds, wind or rough seas, and flights through other hubs can be disrupted. Travel insurance is always sensible.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Combine it with US-dollar pricing and US preclearance at the airport, and Aruba is about as low-risk as a Caribbean date gets.</p>
</GuideArticle>
